A||||8||fierospeeder||GM new car add-ons||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||04-09-2001||11:00 PM||||My dad just recently bought a pontiac sunfire?  anyway, its new.<P>So they talked about the undercoating and paint protection.<P>I was wondering if we can buy the stuff that they talked about.<P>I seen a small clip of them applying a white pasty substance to the car, saying its made out of "teflon" with a electric buffer.  Its guaranteed to last 5 years and protect the car's paint from the sun and acid rain.  <P>Also, they talked about undercoating being done electrostaticly, while in the video it shows a guy doing it the old fashion way.<P>My ford tbird turbo 87', has a "waterbased" type of a undercoating, its clear, and has a slight yellowing.  But the car is nearly rustproof underneath.  I think it came from the factory that way and its sprayed in the trunk interior and some places in the engine compartment.  I was wondering if this is the stuff that Jc whitney sells calling it waterbased undercoating.  It looks a lot better that that black rubber crap.<P>I also read that on the trucks, there is a wax that is melted onto the frame and has a melting point of 300 degrees.  I did notice it and it has a plastic look to it.  To prevent corrosion, it would be kewl to put that on the fieros cradle.<P>any info would be great||||reg||1
Z||000001||LarryB||04-10-2001||09:07 AM||||Most of the dealer-installed/applied addons are little more than highly lucrative gimmicks to boost the dealers profit.  Almost like the extended warranties that stores sell on appliances. :/ (It's typical for those warranties to pay out approximately $3 in claims for every $100 of insurance payments taken in)<P>Improperly applied undercoating and rustproofing makes it more likely, not less, that you'll have problems down the road. Water gets in through breaks and cracks and is trapped, accelerating the rusting process.<P>Keep a good wax on the car and properly wash it regularly, including the wheel wells and underneath the chassis, and you can expect a good long life for the car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||04-10-2001||09:07 AM||||I think that the sole purpose of the dealer add-on paint protectors and undercoating is to add to the dealer's bottom line. <BR>New cars are pretty much as rust-protected as they can get. In fact, some of the "rust proofing" that requires holes to be drilled to spray chemicals in will do more harm than good, as it will compromise the integrity of the factory-applied coatings. Might even void the warranty.<BR>I know you didn't specifically ask about rust-proofing, but it usually gets lumped in there, too.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||TheFiero64||04-10-2001||10:43 AM||||I think Por-15 and Corroless Rust Stabilizer are the only 2 good coatings out there. I would love to take my car apart and cover the whole frame.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||04-10-2001||01:01 PM||||THERE add on paint 'protection' dont do anything for the paint a quick wax job wont do.<P>there undercoating dont stop rust. at best if you read the contract they only pay to repair a rust out 'if' you can prove it was defective application, and they only pay up to the amount you pay for the rustproof. In math thats if both of the door rust out and you paid $200 for the job, theyll only give you $200, that wont replace 2 $500 doors.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||04-10-2001||05:43 PM||||Be careful having that stuff put on a car.  Take a look in the manual, rustproofing voids the corrosion warrenty.  If its as good as the salesman claims, why would the manufacturer not want to stand behind their car with it on?!?  "Cuz its a gimmick, and often will cause rust to form quicker!<P>New cars are built a lot better than they used to be.  We have some of the most rusty cars in the country here, due to the massive amounts of snow and therefore salt on the roads in the winter.  Yet, its common to see cars 10 years old with little or no rust on them.  You will most likely wear out a new car before it rusts out.<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierosig2.JPG"> <BR>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000006||Taijiguy||04-10-2001||06:01 PM||||Glad you pointed that out John. Hard to believe a dealer would actually sell/apply a product that in facts voids the new car warranty. Just another reason I hate new car dealers. Another perfect example of how so often profit is more important than morality in business.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><BR>~Taiji<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||04-10-2001||08:30 PM||||I saw a car that had rustoleum applied 30 years ago when the guy got it home. The frame was nearly rust free. It would have been completely rust free, except for some slight rust due to stone chips and such. For contrast the weatherstrip had dried out and the hood, trunk, and around the windows were all rusted out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JSocha||04-10-2001||09:07 PM||||In 1997 we bought a new 97 Dodge Dually.<P>Had them put the undercoating on, paint sealer and interior protector.<P>Was not impressed with either the paint sealent or the interior protection (had that redone twice cause it did not repel water as they claimed it would).  Really wondering if they did it to begin with.<P>Undercoating, well it seemed to work well.<P>Anyways, finally, after not seeing any improvements with the Paint Sealer, I personally waxed the vehicle on my own one beautiful afternoon shortly after we got it home, had better shine and it repeled water for the summer months!<P>Had the interior cleaned by a Carpet Cleaning service here in town where they put an Industrial/Commercial grade fabric protector on it (carpets and seats) and wouldn't you know, water finally did not soak into the seats, even after I seen them redo it twice before at the dealership and seen them mix the chemacals.<P>We finally traded our 97 dually in February of 2000, after we custom ordered and agreed on the price I was willing to pay for a 2001 Dodge dually custom built from the factory.<P>They didn't offer the undercoating as they informed me that the frame is already coated right at the factory with a new "clear" protectectant and is on most new vehicles sold today.<P>They did however offer the "paint sealant" and the "interior carpet and leather" protection after the truck was delivered to the dealership for "more" on the invoice.  <P>Under one condition that I placed to the dealership when the vehicle finally came in and they wanted to sell me those options, that I would go for it only if they agreed that if I was not impressed or satisfied like the last vehicle they sold me with that put on (as they knew how I felt with the 97 I bought from them), they would have to give me a full refund.<P>Didn't cost us a cent more from the original quote and final invoice once our truck was delivered as they at that point threw both the "paint sealent" and "interior" protection in for nothing, free, nada.<P>Dealership Gimmick?  You bet ya!<P>Ended up waxing the vehicle on my own again when the weather warmed up and went and had the interior done again by our local Carpet Cleaning service and had them throw their protectant on.<P>Now after muddy roads and the wife and kids taking care of the horses at the barn (Horse sh*t stinks BTW on wet carpets), interior needs to be totally redone this summer.<P>If you're buying a new vehicle, spend the money else where and have another reputable service do the undercoating (& make sure it doesn't breach your warranty) and/or interior.<P>The paint sealent, you can purchase from any good car parts store (a small bottle typically goes a long ways) and put that on yourself or a good "carnuba" based wax.<P>Anybody use Turtle Waxes "Tefelon" car wax when they offered it back about 10 years ago?  Cause I did on my '86 Dodge RAM Charger LE 150, '88 and '87 Chrysler LeBaron's and those vehicle shined for months on end and repeled water like there was no tomorrow when it rained or was washed!  Snow and ice wouldn't even stick to the surface.  The surface was as smooth and silky as a newborns bottom.<P>Okay, I think I have put more then my 2 cents in on this subject.<P>However, before I go, if anybody has used the "Tefelon" wax Mfg by Turtle wax and still know's where to get some, please let me know as I only have a little left in my bottle and I want to wax our Fiero with it!||||reg||1
